      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pet hair is where a robot vacuum earns its money, because the problem is continuous and the solution is frequency. It is also where a badly chosen machine fails fastest — hair binds bristle brushes solid in days and fills a half-litre bin in a single run.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Yes, and Better Than You Expect
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pet hair is the single strongest use case for a robot vacuum, and it is the one where the frequency argument does all the work. Hair sheds continuously, accumulates visibly within a day or two on hard floors, and is the thing owners most often feel they are losing a battle with. A machine that removes it every day changes that completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The qualification is that pet hair is also the most demanding material for the mechanical parts of a robot. It wraps around brush bearings, clogs filters faster than household dust, fills a small bin within a run or two, and wound around an axle it can stop a wheel turning.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the answer is: yes, robots handle pet hair well, provided the machine is chosen for it. That means three specific features — an anti-tangle brush design, a self-emptying dock, and enough filtration to hold the dander rather than exhaust it — and a small amount of weekly maintenance that owners without pets can skip.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Choose On the Brush, Not the Suction</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Rubber rollers and anti-tangle designs are what handle pet hair; Pascal figures are not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 4,000 Pa robot with dual rubber rollers collects more hair over a week than an 8,000 Pa machine with a bristle brush that binds solid on day two — because the second one stops working properly between cleanings.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="what-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Picks Up Pet Hair
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three elements of the machine do the work, and only one of them appears prominently in the marketing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brush Design</h3>
          <p>
            Rubber or silicone rollers sweep hair toward the suction path without letting it wind into the bristle base, which is what happens with traditional brush bars. Dual counter-rotating rollers do it best, because the second roller keeps the first clear. This is the single most important specification for a pet household.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Suction Path Width</h3>
          <p>
            Hair forms clumps rather than dispersing like dust, so a wide, unobstructed path from brush to bin matters more than raw suction. Machines with a narrow throat clog at the transition, which shows up as a robot that appears to be working while collecting almost nothing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side Brush Design</h3>
          <p>
            Five-armed and rubber-tipped side brushes flick hair inward without wrapping. Three-armed bristle side brushes are notorious hair collectors, and on a pet household they need cutting free weekly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Suction matters, but it is the third variable rather than the first. Above roughly 4,000 to 5,000 Pa, a good brush design and a clear path determine pickup far more than another few thousand Pascals on the box.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="carpet-vs-hard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Carpet Versus Hard Floors With Pets
          </h2>

          <p>
            The floor type changes the answer more than the pet does.
          </p>

          <p>
            On hard floors — tile, sealed wood, vinyl plank — robots are excellent with pet hair. Hair sits on the surface, the brush sweeps it into the path, and a daily run keeps the floor genuinely clear. Households with hard floors and a shedding dog often describe the robot as the appliance they would replace fastest if it broke.
          </p>

          <p>
            On low-pile carpet, performance is good with rubber rollers. Hair works its way into the pile but stays within reach of an agitating roller, and a daily pass keeps it from building up.
          </p>

          <p>
            On medium and high-pile carpet, robots skim. Hair drives deep into the pile and it takes the sustained agitation of a full-size brush roll to lift it, which is exactly what a robot does not have. In a heavily carpeted house with pets, a robot maintains the surface and a conventional vacuum still does the real work — an arrangement we set out in more detail in our <a href="/robot-vacuum-vs-regular-vacuum/">robot versus regular vacuum comparison</a>.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pet Hair Pickup</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
