                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Low-pile carpet and rugs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rubber rollers make the difference</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Medium-pile carpet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface only; upright still needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-pile and shag</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Robots may not drive on it at all</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="bin-and-dock" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why the Dock Matters More With Pets
          </h2>

          <p>
            A robot's onboard bin holds between a third and half a litre. Pet hair is bulky relative to its weight and fills that faster than any other domestic debris — in a house with a shedding dog, one or two runs is realistic.
          </p>

          <p>
            That single fact is why a self-emptying dock goes from a convenience to close to a requirement in a pet household. Without one, the daily run people bought the robot for means emptying a hair-packed bin every second day, which is both unpleasant and exactly the chore the machine was supposed to remove.
          </p>

          <p>
            A full bin also degrades cleaning before it is visibly full. Airflow through a bin packed with hair falls sharply, so the second half of a run picks up noticeably less than the first. With a dock the robot starts every clean empty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bagged docks are the better choice here for the same reason they suit allergy households: hair and dander stay sealed rather than being released when you tip a canister. Our <a href="/self-emptying-robot-vacuum-worth-it/">dock guide</a> covers the bagged-versus-bagless trade in full.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Maintenance a Pet Household Cannot Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part that decides whether a robot keeps working well in a house with animals. None of it takes long; all of it gets neglected.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut the Brushes Free Weekly</h3>
          <p>
            Even anti-tangle rollers collect hair at the bearing ends. Left there it creates drag, degrades pickup and eventually damages the bearing. Most robots ship with a small cutting tool for exactly this — two minutes a week is enough.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Wheels and Axles</h3>
          <p>
            Hair wraps around wheel axles and the side brush spindle, and a wheel that cannot turn freely produces navigation errors people blame on the sensors. Look underneath every couple of weeks.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Change Filters More Often</h3>
          <p>
            Dander and fine hair load a filter far faster than household dust. Where a manual suggests three months, a pet household should expect six to eight weeks, and a loaded filter costs suction exactly when you need it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Empty the Dock Bag on Time</h3>
          <p>
            A dock bag in a pet home fills in four to six weeks rather than the eight to ten a manual assumes. Once it is full, the dock cannot empty the robot properly and you are back to a machine with a full bin without realising it.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="allergies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pet Allergies: What a Robot Does and Does Not Solve
          </h2>

          <p>
            For someone allergic to a pet, the allergen is not the hair itself — it is dander, saliva proteins and skin flakes carried on and alongside it. That distinction matters when judging what a vacuum achieves.
          </p>

          <p>
            A robot running daily removes a substantial share of the material before it is disturbed and redistributed, which genuinely helps. Frequency is the mechanism: allergen that is taken off the floor on Tuesday is not kicked back into the air on Wednesday.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a robot does less well is contain the finest fraction. Its filtration is smaller and rarely sealed to the standard of a good HEPA upright, so some of what it collects is exhausted. A bagged self-emptying dock helps considerably, because the worst moment for re-release is emptying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The arrangement that works best in an allergy household is layered: a robot for daily removal, a sealed HEPA vacuum weekly for carpet and upholstery, an air purifier in the bedroom, and the pet kept off the bed. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/">allergy purifier guide</a> covers the air side, and it is worth reading alongside this — no floor cleaning strategy captures what is already airborne.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            One more practical point specific to dogs rather than cats: mud. A robot runs into tracked-in mud and paw prints far more often than it runs into anything else seasonal, and a machine with a mop function can genuinely deal with the residue once the solids are up. What it cannot deal with is wet mud, which smears and then dries into the brush. If your dog uses a garden in winter, a doormat that actually works is a better investment than any robot feature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi-pet households should also expect to size up on everything: bin capacity, dock bag change frequency, filter stock and brush spares. Two shedding animals is not twice the hair of one in practical terms — it is roughly twice the hair arriving on a machine whose consumables were specified for a house without any — so buying spares in packs rather than singles saves both money and the week the robot spends waiting on a delivery.
          </p>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Look For in a Pet-Focused Robot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Putting it together, these are the specifications that matter for a shedding household, in order.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dual rubber or anti-tangle rollers.</strong> Non-negotiable. This single feature is the difference between a machine you cut free weekly and one you cut free monthly, and between consistent pickup and gradual decline.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A self-emptying dock, ideally bagged.</strong> Pet hair fills a small bin within a run or two; the dock is what makes daily running practical rather than theoretical.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Good obstacle avoidance.</strong> Pet households have bowls, toys and — occasionally — accidents on the floor. A robot that recognises and avoids pet waste rather than driving through it is worth every penny of the premium, and anyone who has experienced the alternative will confirm that without hesitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A wide, clear suction path and easy brush access.</strong> Hair clumps, so the path matters, and a brush assembly you can remove without tools is one you will actually clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Decent filtration.</strong> A sealed filter and a bagged dock keep dander contained rather than recirculating it. Our <a href="/best-robot-vacuum/">robot vacuum comparison</a> flags which machines handle hair best, and the ones that lead on it are consistently the ones with two rubber rollers rather than the ones with the highest Pascal figures.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Suction Instead of Brush Design</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A high Pascal figure with a bristle brush bar binds solid with hair within days, after which it collects very little. Dual rubber rollers with a lower suction rating out-collect it comfortably over a week because they keep working between cleanings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Without a Self-Emptying Dock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pet hair fills a half-litre bin within one or two runs, so daily cleaning means emptying a hair-packed bin every second day. That is the chore the robot was bought to remove, and it is why pet households rate the dock so highly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Weekly Brush Clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hair at the bearing ends creates drag that degrades pickup gradually enough that owners blame the battery or the motor. Two minutes a week with the supplied cutting tool is the entire maintenance requirement and it keeps the machine performing as new.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing Filters on the Manual's Schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dander and fine hair load filters far faster than ordinary household dust. A manual that says three months means six to eight weeks in a shedding household, and a loaded filter costs suction precisely when there is most to pick up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Deep Clean Carpet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On medium and high-pile carpet a robot removes the surface layer and leaves hair driven into the pile. It is a maintenance machine, not a substitute for the sustained agitation of a proper brush roll, and a carpeted pet home needs both.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums work on pet hair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and pet hair is arguably their strongest use case — daily runs stop hair accumulating in the first place. The qualification is that the robot needs anti-tangle rubber rollers, a self-emptying dock and reasonable filtration, because hair wraps brushes, fills small bins within a run or two, and loads filters quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of robot vacuum is best for pet hair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One with dual rubber or silicone rollers rather than bristle brushes, a self-emptying bagged dock, good obstacle avoidance, and a brush assembly that comes out without tools. Suction matters less than brush design above roughly 4,000 to 5,000 Pa — a well-designed brush collects more over a week than a higher Pascal figure with a bristle bar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums get clogged with pet hair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can, and it is the main maintenance task in a pet household. Hair wraps around brush bearings, wheel axles and the side brush spindle. Cutting it free every week or two with the supplied tool keeps pickup at full strength; leaving it creates drag, degrades cleaning and eventually damages bearings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums work on pet hair in carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On low-pile carpet, yes, particularly with rubber rollers. On medium and high-pile carpet they remove the surface layer and leave hair driven into the pile, because lifting that requires sustained agitation from a full-size brush roll. A carpeted pet household still needs a conventional vacuum for the deep clean.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do I need to empty a robot vacuum with pets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Without a dock, every one or two runs — pet hair is bulky and fills a half-litre bin quickly. With a self-emptying dock, every four to six weeks, which is sooner than the eight to ten weeks manuals assume. A full dock bag means the robot stops being emptied properly without any obvious sign.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are robot vacuums good for pet allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They help, mainly through frequency — allergen removed daily is not re-disturbed the next day. Their filtration is less well sealed than a good HEPA upright, so pair a robot with a bagged dock for containment, a sealed HEPA vacuum weekly, and an air purifier in the bedroom for the airborne fraction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a robot vacuum handle a cat litter tray area?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reasonably well for tracked litter on hard floors, which is one of the more welcome uses. Set the tray area as a priority zone if your app supports it, expect the filter to load faster from the dust, and use a machine with good obstacle avoidance so it routes around the tray rather than into it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

